#!args --files apps/viewtest/tests/files
# viewtest: an app chooses the library's own file dialog (FD_BUILTIN), as a browser whose "Save
# as" looks and behaves the same on every system.  Command 9 opens it in the folder of --files
# with the second type ("dat") selected.  Headless runs always show the own dialog; FD_BUILTIN
# makes windows on Windows, Linux, macOS and Android show it too (before, only the environment
# variable jfgui_FILEDLG=builtin did, for every dialog of the run).  The flag leaves the type's
# index in bits 8..15 alone: a name without an extension gets the second type's.  The dialog's
# name box is #1179660 (id + 12).
cmd 9
expect dialog:"Speichern unter" exists
expect "a.txt" exists
focus #1179660
type "neu"
click button:"Speichern"
expect dialog:"Speichern unter" missing
expect text:"Dateien: 1 neu.dat" exists
# again, with a name that has its extension: it stays
cmd 9
expect dialog:"Speichern unter" exists
focus #1179660
type "notiz.txt"
click button:"Speichern"
expect text:"Dateien: 1 notiz.txt" exists
expect_nowarn
